From a14d0d40230b4730a2cb240937aff16f63f8d83c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 01:38:51 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Assembled and global stiffness matrices: setup shared between
 the host threads

At the first assembly of the tangent operator by kernels, the pattern of the
local matrix and the tables of the global matrix are built on the host. For
the quadratic tetrahedra of a polycrystal with 4.9 M unknowns per process
(435 M entries), this took 10 of the 14 s of this assembly on one core, the
23 other cores of the process and the GPU waiting.

These loops are now shared between the threads of the host
(`forEachThreadRange`, option `enable-openmp`):

- `batch::makeAssembledStiffnessMatrix`: the nodes of the elements are
  checked, the nodes coupled to each node gathered and sorted, and the rows
  of the components of each node written with their null values, by ranges
  of elements and of nodes. The coupled nodes of a node are written at its
  place in an array which has room for the nodes of all its elements: a
  thread does not need the number of nodes coupled to the previous ones.
  The offsets of the rows are then a sum over the rows;
- `batch::GlobalStiffnessMatrix`: the rows of the global matrix are shared
  between the threads, each one with its rows of the local matrix. The
  pattern of the global matrix is copied once and shared, each thread
  searching the entries with its own finder, whose row is selected once
  per row of the local matrix. The entries of the rows owned by other
  processes are written at their place in the entries sent, which follows
  from the rows before them. The lists of the contributions are built by
  counting, without second copy of the offsets: the first contribution to
  each entry is kept in its next offset, which ends as the offset of the
  next entry;
- the copies of the blocks of hypre are first written by the threads: the
  copy from a GPU took several times longer when it also mapped the pages
  of its destination.

The matrix giving the pattern of the global matrix is now built where hypre
computes: on a GPU, its arrays are copied from the ones of the local matrix
on the GPU and its values set by a kernel, instead of being copied to the
host, set there, and copied back.

The matrices and the tables are the same, bit for bit, whatever the number
of threads. At this size, on a MI300A and its 24 cores, the first assembly
takes 5.5 s against 13.8 s, and 12.3 s with one thread.

GradientsKernelsTest: the columns following the diagonal entry of each row
are sorted, which, with the pattern of MFEM, fixes the arrays of the
matrix; with the option, the test is also run with 7 threads (more than
the elements of the smallest meshes). GlobalStiffnessMatrixTest: with the
option, the checksums of the global matrices are the same with one and 5
threads, on the host and on a GPU.
